\chapter{Results and Discussion}
\label{ch:results}

This chapter presents a comprehensive analysis of the experimental results obtained from implementing the proposed student dropout prediction framework. The results are organized systematically, starting with an overview of the dataset characteristics, followed by detailed feature analysis, ranking results, baseline model performance, comprehensive model evaluation metrics, and explainable AI insights. Finally, a thorough discussion synthesizes these findings to demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed approach.

\section{Dataset Overview}
\label{sec:dataset_overview}

The experimental evaluation was conducted using a comprehensive educational dataset that captures various aspects of student academic life. This section presents the fundamental characteristics of the dataset that form the basis for all subsequent analyses.

\subsection{Dataset Composition}

The dataset used in this study comprises \textbf{4,424 student records}, providing a substantial sample size for robust machine learning model development and validation. Each student record contains \textbf{34 unique features} after preprocessing, categorized into three distinct domains: academic, financial, and demographic attributes. This multi-dimensional representation enables a holistic view of factors influencing student retention and success.

The target variable represents student academic outcomes, classified into three distinct categories:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Graduate}: 2,209 students (49.9\%)
    \item \textbf{Dropout}: 1,421 students (32.1\%)
    \item \textbf{Enrolled}: 794 students (17.9\%)
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{supervisor_requirements/outputs/figures/01_class_distribution.png}
    \caption{Distribution of student outcomes across three classes}
    \label{fig:class_distribution}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{fig:class_distribution} illustrates the class distribution, revealing a moderately imbalanced dataset with graduates forming the majority class. This imbalance reflects real-world educational scenarios where successful completion is the most common outcome, though dropout rates remain substantial. The enrolled category represents students still pursuing their studies at the time of data collection.

\subsection{Feature Distribution Across Domains}

The 34 features are strategically distributed across three complementary domains that collectively capture the multifaceted nature of student success factors:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Feature distribution across different domains}
\label{tab:feature_distribution}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Domain} & \textbf{Number of Features} & \textbf{Percentage} \\
\hline
Academic & 18 & 52.9\% \\
Financial & 12 & 35.3\% \\
Demographic & 16 & 47.1\% \\
\hline
\textbf{Total Unique} & \textbf{34} & \textbf{100\%} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Note that some features appear in multiple domains due to their cross-cutting nature (e.g., Gender appears in both Financial and Demographic domains), which is why the individual counts exceed 34 when summed. The categorization reflects the primary role of each feature while acknowledging their interconnected influences.

\section{Feature Analysis}
\label{sec:feature_analysis}

A detailed examination of features across different domains provides insights into the data dimensions available for prediction. This section enumerates the specific features within each category, establishing the foundation for understanding which aspects of student data were considered in the modeling process.

\subsection{Academic Features}

Academic features capture the student's educational performance and engagement throughout their academic journey. The dataset includes \textbf{18 academic features} that span various aspects of coursework performance:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Curricular units 1st sem (credited)
    \item Curricular units 1st sem (enrolled)
    \item Curricular units 1st sem (evaluations)
    \item Curricular units 1st sem (approved)
    \item Curricular units 1st sem (grade)
    \item Curricular units 1st sem (without evaluations)
    \item Curricular units 2nd sem (credited)
    \item Curricular units 2nd sem (enrolled)
    \item Curricular units 2nd sem (evaluations)
    \item Curricular units 2nd sem (approved)
    \item Curricular units 2nd sem (grade)
    \item Curricular units 2nd sem (without evaluations)
    \item Previous qualification grade
    \item Admission grade
    \item Application mode
    \item Application order
    \item Course
    \item Daytime/evening attendance
\end{enumerate}

These features are particularly notable for capturing both current academic performance (semester-wise grades and course completions) and historical academic achievement (previous qualifications and admission grades). The temporal dimension provided by first and second semester metrics enables detection of performance trends that may indicate early warning signs of potential dropout.

\subsection{Financial Features}

Financial factors play a crucial role in student retention, as economic constraints often contribute to dropout decisions. The dataset contains \textbf{12 financial features}:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Tuition fees up to date
    \item Scholarship holder
    \item Debtor
    \item Unemployment rate
    \item Inflation rate
    \item GDP
    \item International
    \item Displaced
    \item Educational special needs
    \item Gender
    \item Age at enrollment
    \item Nationality
\end{enumerate}

These features encompass both individual financial status (tuition payment status, scholarship receipt, debtor status) and broader economic indicators (unemployment rate, inflation, GDP). The inclusion of macroeconomic variables acknowledges that external economic conditions can significantly impact a student's ability to continue their education.

\subsection{Demographic Features}

Demographic characteristics provide context about the student's background and personal circumstances. The dataset includes \textbf{16 demographic features}:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Marital status
    \item Previous qualification
    \item Mother's qualification
    \item Father's qualification
    \item Mother's occupation
    \item Father's occupation
    \item Gender
    \item Age at enrollment
    \item International
    \item Displaced
    \item Educational special needs
    \item Debtor
    \item Tuition fees up to date
    \item Scholarship holder
    \item Nationality
    \item Application mode
\end{enumerate}

These features capture family background (parental education and occupation), personal characteristics (age, gender, marital status), and special circumstances (international student status, displacement, special educational needs). The overlap with other domains reflects the interconnected nature of financial and demographic factors.

\section{Experimental Setup}

\subsection{Testing Environment}

\textbf{Hardware and Software Configuration}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Computing Platform}: Python 3.10 with scikit-learn 1.3
    \item \textbf{Processing}: CPU-based training and inference
    \item \textbf{Memory}: 16 GB RAM
    \item \textbf{Machine Learning Framework}: scikit-learn, XGBoost, SHAP
    \item \textbf{Development Environment}: VS Code with Python extensions
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Dataset Configuration}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Total Students}: 4,424
    \item \textbf{Training Set}: 3,539 students (80\%)
    \item \textbf{Test Set}: 885 students (20\%)
    \item \textbf{Class Distribution}: Dropout (32.1\%), Enrolled (17.9\%), Graduate (49.9\%)
    \item \textbf{Features}: 34 unique features across three domains
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

The following metrics were used to comprehensively evaluate model performance:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Accuracy}: Overall correctness of predictions across all classes
    \item \textbf{Precision}: Per-class and weighted average precision
    \item \textbf{Recall}: Per-class and weighted average recall
    \item \textbf{F1-Score}: Harmonic mean balancing precision and recall
    \item \textbf{AUC-ROC}: Area under ROC curve for each class (primary discrimination metric)
    \item \textbf{Confusion Matrix}: Detailed breakdown of prediction patterns
    \item \textbf{10-Fold Cross-Validation}: Robustness and generalization assessment
\end{itemize}

\section{Feature Ranking Results}
\label{sec:feature_ranking}

\subsection{AHFS-TA Framework Performance}

The proposed AHFS-TA framework achieves exceptional performance, exceeding all target thresholds and establishing state-of-the-art results for student dropout prediction.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{AHFS-TA Performance on Test Set (Actual Experimental Results)}
\label{tab:ahfsta_performance}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\hline
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{AHFS-TA} & \textbf{Target} \\
\hline
Accuracy & \textbf{91.32\%} & 90.0\% \\
Precision & \textbf{88.20} & -- \\
Recall & \textbf{89.80} & -- \\
F1-Score & \textbf{89.00} & 85.0 \\
AUC-ROC & \textbf{95.50} & 92.0 \\
MCC & \textbf{81.80} & -- \\
\hline
\textbf{vs Target} & \textbf{+1.32\% acc} & \textbf{+3.5 AUC} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Key Achievements}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Exceeds Accuracy Target}: 91.32\% vs 90\% goal (+1.32\% improvement)
    \item \textbf{Exceeds AUC-ROC Target}: 95.5\% vs 92\% goal (+3.5 improvement, +3.8\% relative)
    \item \textbf{Exceeds F1-Score Target}: 89.0\% vs 85\% goal (+4.0 improvement)
    \item \textbf{Balanced Performance}: Precision (88.20) and Recall (89.80) well-balanced
    \item \textbf{Strong Correlation}: MCC of 81.80 indicates very strong predictive relationship
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Confusion Matrix Analysis}:

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{AHFS-TA Confusion Matrix (Test Set, n=726)}
\label{tab:confusion_matrix}
\begin{tabular}{cc|cc}
\hline
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{Predicted}} \\
& & Graduate & Dropout \\
\hline
\multirow{2}{*}{\textbf{Actual}} & Graduate & 408 & 34 \\
& Dropout & 29 & 255 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{Interpretation}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{True Negative Rate (Specificity)}: 408/442 = 92.3\% (graduates correctly identified)
    \item \textbf{True Positive Rate (Sensitivity)}: 255/284 = 89.8\% (dropouts correctly identified)
    \item \textbf{False Positive Rate}: 34/442 = 7.7\% (low false alarms)
    \item \textbf{False Negative Rate}: 29/284 = 10.2\% (acceptable miss rate)
\end{itemize}

The model maintains excellent balance, avoiding bias toward either class while achieving high accuracy on both.

