    \item \textbf{Larger Dataset}: 3,630 students provides robust validation
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Quantitative and Qualitative Comparison}

\textbf{Quantitative Superiority}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Exceeds all published benchmarks in accuracy and AUC-ROC
    \item Maintains balanced precision-recall (unlike some baselines biased to majority class)
    \item Achieves improvements with feature reduction (efficiency gain)
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Qualitative Advantages}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Multimodal}: First to integrate LLM psychosocial features with traditional metrics
    \item \textbf{Temporal}: Only model with semester-wise progression modeling
    \item \textbf{Adaptive}: Dynamic feature selection during training
    \item \textbf{Explainable}: SHAP values and attention weights provide interpretability
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Areas of Improvement over Baselines}

Compared to traditional machine learning baselines:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{vs Logistic Regression}: +0.22 AUC-ROC improvement with 26\% fewer features
    \item \textbf{vs Random Forest}: Better balanced metrics, temporal awareness
    \item \textbf{vs Gradient Boosting}: Superior interpretability through attention mechanism
    \item \textbf{vs Neural Network}: +2.89\% accuracy, structured architecture
\end{itemize}

Compared to deep learning baselines:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Multimodal advantage}: LLM features provide +1.71\% gain
    \item \textbf{Temporal advantage}: Sequential modeling provides +1.18\% gain
    \item \textbf{Selection advantage}: Adaptive features provide +0.69\% gain
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Research Questions Addressed}

\textbf{RQ1: Can multimodal learning improve dropout prediction?}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Answer}: Yes, conclusively validated
    \item \textbf{Evidence}: LLM features contribute +1.71\% (40\% of total improvement)
    \item \textbf{Statistical Support}: All LLM features significant at p < 0.001
\end{itemize}

\textbf{RQ2: Does temporal modeling enhance prediction accuracy?}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Answer}: Yes, substantial improvement observed
    \item \textbf{Evidence}: Temporal attention adds +1.18\% accuracy
    \item \textbf{Practical Value}: Identifies critical semesters for intervention
\end{itemize}

\textbf{RQ3: Can adaptive selection improve efficiency without sacrificing accuracy?}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Answer}: Yes, dual benefit achieved
    \item \textbf{Evidence}: 26\% feature reduction + +0.69\% accuracy gain
    \item \textbf{Implication}: More interpretable, computationally efficient model
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Theoretical Implications}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Multimodal Learning}: Demonstrates that combining structured data with LLM-derived psychosocial features captures complementary patterns
    \item \textbf{Temporal Dependencies}: Validates importance of modeling educational trajectories as sequences, not static snapshots
    \item \textbf{Adaptive Architectures}: Shows meta-ranking from multiple perspectives (SHAP + LLM + Temporal) outperforms single-stream selection
    \item \textbf{Transfer Learning}: DistilBERT pre-training transfers effectively to educational domain
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Practical Implications}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Early Intervention}: 91.32\% accuracy enables reliable identification of at-risk students
    \item \textbf{Resource Allocation}: 89.8\% recall ensures most dropout-prone students receive support
    \item \textbf{Low False Alarms}: 88.2\% precision minimizes unnecessary interventions
    \item \textbf{Interpretability}: SHAP and attention weights explain predictions to educators
    \item \textbf{Scalability}: Efficient 28-feature model suitable for real-time deployment
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Limitations and Sources of Error}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dataset Scope}: Single institution dataset (3,630 students) - generalization to other universities needs validation
    \item \textbf{Binary Classification}: Removed "Enrolled" class - future work could address 3-class prediction
    \item \textbf{Feature Engineering}: LLM features derived from available text - richer data (essays, forum posts) could improve further
    \item \textbf{Temporal Resolution}: 4-semester sequences - finer granularity (monthly) could capture more dynamics
    \item \textbf{Computational Cost}: DistilBERT requires GPU for training - lighter models needed for resource-constrained settings
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Alternative Interpretations}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{LLM Feature Effectiveness}: Could be dataset-specific - validation on multiple institutions recommended
    \item \textbf{Temporal Contribution}: +1.18\% gain might vary with sequence length (4 semesters chosen empirically)
    \item \textbf{Feature Selection Timing}: Epoch 5 selection empirical - adaptive online selection could be explored
\end{itemize}

\section{Summary}

This chapter presented comprehensive experimental results validating the AHFS-TA framework:

\textbf{Key Findings}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Exceeds All Targets}: 91.32\% accuracy (+1.32\%), 95.5\% AUC-ROC (+3.8\%)
    \item \textbf{State-of-the-Art}: Outperforms all baselines and published benchmarks
    \item \textbf{Component Validation}: Ablation study quantifies each contribution
    \item \textbf{Multimodal Advantage}: LLM features provide 40\% of improvement
    \item \textbf{Feature Efficiency}: 26\% reduction while improving performance
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Main Contributions}:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item First validated application of DistilBERT for educational psychosocial feature extraction
    \item Novel three-stream adaptive feature selection combining SHAP + LLM + Temporal perspectives
    \item State-of-the-art performance exceeding all published work by +4.02\% accuracy
    \item Rigorous ablation demonstrating additive gains from all components
\end{enumerate}

The results establish AHFS-TA as a significant advancement in educational outcome prediction, providing both superior predictive performance and interpretable insights for early intervention systems. The next chapter concludes with broader implications and future research directions.

